    {
      "id": "el6jhdk",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1.2 Medieval Period"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3ql8nqi",
      "content": "The Katyuri Dynasty (7th–11th CE) was the first major power to unify Kumaon. Their capital was Kartikeyapura (modern Baijnath). They built magnificent temples including the Baijnath temple complex. The dynasty declined following internal conflicts and was replaced by smaller principalities."
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Formation of Uttarakhand State",
      "id": "oc2qj4f"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nkqn74z",
      "content": "The Chand Dynasty of Kumaon: Raja Som Chand founded this dynasty c. 700–900 CE with capital at Champawat. The dynasty later shifted the capital to Almora (16th century under Balo Kalyan Chand). Chand rulers were great patrons of art; Kumaoni painting tradition flourished under them. Baj Bahadur Chand was the last independent Chand ruler."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "t80fugd",
      "content": "The Parmar (Panwar) Dynasty of Garhwal: Founded around 888 CE by Kanak Pal at Chandpur Garhi (near Pauri). The dynasty unified the 52 Garhwal principalities (Bawan Garh) by Raja Ajay Pal in the early 16th century. Capital was later moved to Srinagar on the Alaknanda. Garhwal was also known as 'Srinagar Kingdom'."
    },
    {
      "id": "kq7fjpa",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1.3 Gurkha Invasion and British Period"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "yn4hw3b",
      "content": "The Gurkhas (Nepal) invaded and conquered Kumaon in 1790 under Amar Singh Thapa and occupied Garhwal in 1804. The Gurkha rule (1804–1815) was harsh — forced labour (Begar), heavy taxes, and displacement. Raja Pradyumna Shah of Garhwal died fighting the Gurkhas at Khurbura near Dehra Dun in 1804."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "tsytbuq",
      "content": "The Anglo-Nepalese War (1814–1816) ended Gurkha dominance. The Treaty of Sugauli (1816) gave Kumaon and the eastern part of Garhwal to the British East India Company. Western Garhwal was restored to Sudarshan Shah (son of Pradyumna Shah) as the Tehri Garhwal princely state."
    },
